---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.
Ans : The current thread's wake-up time is calculated when timer_sleep() is called, adding the sleep duration to the current ticks. After that thread is added to sleep_list and blocked using a semaphore (sema_down). When the thread's sleep_until is greater than or equal to the current time, the timer interrupt handler  removes the thread from the list and calls sema_up() to wake it up.


>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?
Ans : Only the sleep_list is iterated over by the timer interrupt handler to see whether any threads need to be woken up. It performs the simple task of comparing each thread's sleep_until value with the current tick value. A thread is removed from the list and sema_up() is called if its time has expired.  By focusing just on the necessary tasks, this prevents the interrupt handler from performing excessive processing .


---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?
Ans : Changing the sleep_list in timer_sleep() and timer_interrupt(), race situations can be prevented by disabling interrupts and before adding the thread to the sleep_list in timer_sleep(), interrupts are disabled using intr_disable() and then re-enabled. This makes sure that no two threads can change the sleep_list at the same time.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?
Ans: Race conditions are avoided by using the same interrupt disabling mechanism i.e.intr_disable() in both timer_sleep() and the timer interrupt handler (timer_interrupt()). This ensures atomicity in list operations by preventing the interrupt handler from running while a thread is being added to the sleep_list.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?
Ans: This design is effective since it makes use of a list of sleeping threads (sleep_list) to handle several sleeping threads in a simple way.  It saves CPU resources by allowing threads to sleep without busy-waiting by using semaphores .  In order to minimize the amount of work done on each tick, the interrupt handler just reviews the list of sleeping threads. Because it uses less CPU power and scales effectively with the number of threads, this design is better than ones that would employ busy-wait loops or constantly polling all threads.

